# Environment variables for the MCP workshop
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your API key:
#
#   cp .env.example .env

# =============================================================================
# LLM provider (REQUIRED)
# =============================================================================
#
# Three variables decide WHO we talk to. The agent code contains no vendor
# name anywhere: it speaks an OpenAI-COMPATIBLE API, which several providers
# expose. Swapping provider is therefore a configuration change, not a code
# change.
#
# That is not a theoretical point. This workshop ran on GitHub Models until
# 30 July 2026, when the service was shut down with six weeks' notice and no
# migration path. The migration was a key, a URL and a model name - the three
# lines just below.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECOMMENDED FOR THE WORKSHOP: Google AI Studio (Gemini)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   1. Go to https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#   2. Sign in with the Google account you already have
#   3. "Create API key" - under a minute, no credit card
#   4. Paste the key into OPENAI_API_KEY below
#
#   The context window is why we picked Gemini: the tool manifest and the
#   weather data sit in the conversation history and get sent along on every
#   call, so a tight tokens-per-minute limit throttles you long before the
#   daily quota runs out.
#
#   MODEL CHOICE: a LITE model, not the newest one. Measured 2026-08-23,
#   5 calls each, same prompt and same tool definition:
#
#     model                     ok     called the tool   median
#     ------------------------  -----  ---------------   --------
#     gemini-3.5-flash-lite     5/5    5/5                 598 ms
#     gemini-3.1-flash-lite     5/5    5/5                 600 ms
#     gemini-flash-lite-latest  5/5    5/5                 615 ms
#     gemini-flash-latest       4/5    4/5 (one 503)      3112 ms
#     gemini-3.7-flash          3/5    3/5 (429, throttled) 4516 ms
#
#   The newest models are the ones everybody is hammering. Free-tier quotas
#   say the same thing: the lite models get 500 requests/day and 15/minute,
#   the plain Flash models get 20/day.
#
#   We do not need the best model here. We need one that is good enough and
#   that answers.
#
OPENAI_API_KEY=your-gemini-api-key-here
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai/
OPENAI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALTERNATIVES - comment out the three lines above and use these instead
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OpenAI:
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
#   OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
#
# Groq:
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
#   OPENAI_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile
#   NOTE: the free tier is ~6 000 tokens/minute. With the tool manifest and
#         weather data riding along in the history, that is two or three
#         calls per minute before you are throttled.
#
# A local model via Ollama - no key, no network:
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1
#   OPENAI_MODEL=llama3.1
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=ollama
#
# GitHub Models: RETIRED 30 July 2026. The endpoint answers 410.

# Weather data comes from yr.no (api.met.no) - no API key needed.
# https://api.met.no/weatherapi/locationforecast/2.0/documentation

# =============================================================================
# Optional settings
# =============================================================================

# Log level (DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR)
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Where each service tees its log inside its container. Leave this unset unless
# you have a reason to move it: each service defaults to its own file under
# /app/logs/, and setting it here would point all three at the same one.
# LOG_FILE=/app/logs/mcp-server.log

# Allowed browser origins for the MCP server, comma-separated.
# localhost and *.app.github.dev (Codespaces) are always allowed.
# A value of "*" disables the check - handy when debugging, but you lose the
# DNS rebinding protection with it.
# MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# LAB 3 needs no key. The news tool reads Google News RSS, which is open:
# no signup, any topic, and it answers XML rather than JSON - which is the
# point. The MCP envelope is yours to build whatever the source speaks.

# =============================================================================
# Service URLs (internal Docker networking - do not change)
# =============================================================================
MCP_SERVER_URL=http://mcp-server:8000
AGENT_SERVICE_URL=http://travel-agent:8001

# NOTE: COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME is deliberately NOT set here.
#
# It used to be `mcp-workshop`, while the checkout directory is
# `mcp-lab-jz26-final`. That meant `docker compose` WITHOUT this file used one
# project name and WITH it used another - two separate projects, each with
# its own network and volumes.
#
# On its own that would be harmless, but every service in
# docker-compose.yml has an explicit `container_name`, and those are GLOBAL
# rather than project-scoped. So the two projects collide:
#
#   Conflict. The container name "/travel-weather-mcp" is already in use
#
# Leaving it unset means the project name is always the directory name,
# with or without this file. If you hit the conflict on an older checkout,
# run `make clean`.
